/* AI brain Q&A pack 02: science & space */
(window.AIQA = window.AIQA || []).push(
  ["what is gravity | how does gravity work | define gravity | explain gravity", "Gravity is the force that pulls objects with mass toward each other. The more mass something has, the stronger its pull, which is why Earth keeps us on the ground and the Sun keeps the planets in orbit."],
  ["who discovered gravity | who found out about gravity | who invented gravity", "Nobody invented gravity, but **Isaac Newton** described it mathematically in 1687 with his law of universal gravitation. Later, Albert Einstein's general relativity (1915) explained gravity as the bending of space and time by mass."],
  ["did an apple really fall on newton's head | newton apple story | was the apple story true", "Newton said that watching an apple fall from a tree helped inspire his thinking about gravity. There's no good evidence it actually hit him on the head, though; that part is probably a fun exaggeration."],
  ["why do things fall down | why do objects fall | why does stuff fall to the ground", "Things fall because Earth's gravity pulls them toward its center. Near the surface, falling objects speed up by about 9.8 meters per second every second, if air resistance doesn't slow them."],
  ["do heavy objects fall faster than light ones | does a feather fall slower than a hammer | do heavier things fall faster", "Without air, all objects fall at the same rate no matter their weight. A feather falls slower on Earth only because air pushes against it. Astronaut David Scott famously dropped a hammer and a feather on the Moon, and they landed together."],
  ["what is the acceleration due to gravity | what is g in physics | how fast do things accelerate when falling", "On Earth's surface, gravity makes falling objects accelerate at about **9.8 m/s²** (often rounded to 10). That means a falling object gains about 9.8 meters per second of speed every second."],
  ["what is the difference between mass and weight | mass vs weight | is mass the same as weight", "Mass is how much matter something contains and stays the same everywhere. Weight is the force of gravity pulling on that mass, so it changes depending on where you are. On the Moon you'd weigh about one-sixth as much, but your mass would be the same."],
  ["how much would i weigh on the moon | weight on the moon | why do you weigh less on the moon", "You'd weigh about **one-sixth** of your Earth weight on the Moon, because the Moon has much less mass and weaker gravity. Someone whose scale reads 60 kg on Earth would read about 10 kg on the Moon."],
  ["is there gravity in space | why do astronauts float | is there zero gravity in space", "There is gravity in space! Astronauts on the International Space Station float because they and the station are constantly falling around Earth together, which is called free fall. At the ISS's height, gravity is still about 90% as strong as on the ground."],
  ["what is an orbit | how do orbits work | how do satellites stay up", "An orbit is the curved path one object takes around another because of gravity. A satellite moves sideways so fast that as it falls toward Earth, the ground curves away beneath it, so it keeps falling around the planet instead of into it."],
  ["what are newton's laws of motion | newton's three laws | explain newton's laws", "Newton's three laws of motion are:\n1. An object stays at rest or keeps moving in a straight line unless a force acts on it (inertia).\n2. Force equals mass times acceleration (F = ma).\n3. For every action there is an equal and opposite reaction."],
  ["what is inertia | define inertia | what does inertia mean", "Inertia is an object's tendency to keep doing what it's already doing: staying still or moving at the same speed in the same direction. It's why you lurch forward when a car stops suddenly. More mass means more inertia."],
  ["what is friction | how does friction work | define friction", "Friction is a force that resists motion when two surfaces rub against each other. It lets your shoes grip the floor and brakes stop a bike, but it also wastes energy as heat. Rough surfaces usually create more friction than smooth ones."],
  ["what is force | what is a force in physics | define force", "A force is a push or a pull that can change how an object moves or its shape. Forces are measured in **newtons (N)**. Examples include gravity, friction, magnetism and the push of your hand."],
  ["what is energy | define energy | what is energy in science", "Energy is the ability to do work or cause change. It comes in many forms, like motion (kinetic), stored (potential), heat, light, sound, chemical and electrical energy. It's measured in **joules**."],
  ["can energy be created or destroyed | what is the law of conservation of energy | conservation of energy", "No. The law of conservation of energy says energy can't be created or destroyed, only changed from one form to another. For example, a battery turns chemical energy into electrical energy, and a light bulb turns that into light and heat."],
  ["what is kinetic energy | define kinetic energy", "Kinetic energy is the energy an object has because it's moving. The faster and heavier something is, the more kinetic energy it has. A rolling bowling ball has more kinetic energy than a rolling marble at the same speed."],
  ["what is potential energy | define potential energy | what is stored energy", "Potential energy is stored energy an object has because of its position or condition. A book on a high shelf has gravitational potential energy, and a stretched rubber band has elastic potential energy. When released, it turns into kinetic energy."],
  ["what is the difference between speed and velocity | speed vs velocity", "Speed tells you how fast something is going. Velocity tells you how fast **and** in which direction. A car going 50 km/h around a curve has a constant speed but a changing velocity."],
  ["what is acceleration | define acceleration", "Acceleration is how quickly velocity changes. Speeding up, slowing down and changing direction are all forms of acceleration. It's measured in meters per second squared (m/s²)."],
  ["what is momentum | define momentum", "Momentum is mass times velocity: it measures how hard it is to stop a moving object. A slow truck can have more momentum than a fast baseball because it's so much heavier."],
  ["what is pressure | define pressure in science", "Pressure is how much force is pushing on a certain area. The same force spread over a big area creates low pressure, while a small area creates high pressure. That's why snowshoes keep you from sinking and a sharp knife cuts easily."],
  ["why do things float | why do some things float and others sink | what is buoyancy", "Things float when they are less dense than the liquid they're in. Water pushes up on objects with a force equal to the weight of the water they push aside; this is called buoyancy. If that push is bigger than the object's weight, it floats."],
  ["how do huge ships float | how does a metal ship float | why doesn't a boat sink", "A steel ship floats because its hollow shape pushes aside a huge amount of water. The ship plus the air inside it is less dense overall than water, so the upward buoyant force can match its weight."],
  ["what is density | define density | how do you calculate density", "Density is how much mass is packed into a given space. You find it by dividing mass by volume. Lead is very dense, while foam is not, so a small lead ball can weigh more than a big chunk of foam."],
  ["what is light | what is light made of | how does light work", "Light is a form of energy that travels as electromagnetic waves. It also behaves like tiny packets of energy called **photons**. The light we can see is only a small part of a much bigger spectrum that includes radio waves, X-rays and more."],
  ["how fast is the speed of light | what is the speed of light | how fast does light travel", "Light travels at about **300,000 km per second** (299,792 km/s exactly) in empty space. That's fast enough to circle Earth about 7.5 times in one second."],
  ["can anything go faster than light | is the speed of light the fastest | can we travel faster than light", "According to Einstein's theory of relativity, nothing with mass can reach or exceed the speed of light in a vacuum. It's the universe's ultimate speed limit for matter, energy and information."],
  ["how long does light take to reach earth from the sun | how long does sunlight take to get here | how old is the sunlight we see", "Sunlight takes about **8 minutes and 20 seconds** to travel the roughly 150 million km from the Sun to Earth. So you always see the Sun as it was about 8 minutes ago."],
  ["what is the electromagnetic spectrum | types of electromagnetic waves | what are em waves", "The electromagnetic spectrum is the full range of light-like waves, from longest to shortest:\n- Radio waves\n- Microwaves\n- Infrared\n- Visible light\n- Ultraviolet\n- X-rays\n- Gamma rays\nThey all travel at the speed of light but carry different amounts of energy."],
  ["what are the colors of the rainbow | rainbow colors in order | what is roygbiv", "The colors of the rainbow in order are red, orange, yellow, green, blue, indigo and violet, often remembered as **ROYGBIV**. Red has the longest wavelength and violet the shortest."],
  ["why is the sky blue | what makes the sky blue | why is the sky blue during the day", "Sunlight contains all colors, but when it hits air molecules, blue light gets scattered much more than red because its waves are shorter. That scattered blue light comes at you from every direction, so the whole sky looks blue. This is called **Rayleigh scattering**."],
  ["why are sunsets red and orange | why is the sky red at sunset | why is the sunset orange", "At sunset, sunlight travels through much more air to reach you. Most of the blue light gets scattered away along the way, leaving the reds, oranges and pinks to reach your eyes."],
  ["how are rainbows made | how does a rainbow form | why do rainbows appear", "Rainbows form when sunlight shines into raindrops. Each drop bends (refracts) the light, reflects it off the back, and bends it again, splitting white light into its colors. You see one when the Sun is behind you and rain is in front of you."],
  ["what is a double rainbow | why are there two rainbows", "A double rainbow happens when light reflects twice inside the raindrops. The second, fainter rainbow appears outside the first, and its colors are in reverse order, with red on the inside."],
  ["what is refraction | define refraction | why does a straw look bent in water", "Refraction is the bending of light as it passes from one material into another, like from water into air. It's why a straw in a glass of water looks bent or broken, and it's how lenses in glasses and cameras focus light."],
  ["what is reflection | how do mirrors work | why can i see myself in a mirror", "Reflection is when light bounces off a surface. Mirrors are very smooth and shiny, so they reflect light in an orderly way, creating a clear image. Rough surfaces scatter light in all directions instead."],
  ["what is a prism | what does a prism do", "A prism is a clear, angled piece of glass or plastic that bends light. Because each color bends by a slightly different amount, a prism splits white light into a rainbow of colors. Isaac Newton used prisms to show that white light is a mix of all colors."],
  ["why is white light white | what color is light | is white a color", "White light is a mix of all the colors of the visible spectrum together. When something looks white, it's reflecting all those colors back to your eyes. Black is what you see when an object absorbs nearly all the light."],
  ["why do we see colors | how do we see color | why is a leaf green", "Objects absorb some colors of light and reflect others. A leaf looks green because it absorbs most red and blue light and reflects green light into your eyes. Special cells in your eyes called cones then detect those colors."],
  ["what is a laser | how do lasers work", "A laser produces a narrow, powerful beam of light where all the light waves are the same color and move in step. Lasers are used in barcode scanners, surgery, fiber-optic internet and much more. Never point one at anyone's eyes!"],
  ["what is sound | how does sound work | what is sound made of", "Sound is a vibration that travels as a wave through a material like air, water or solids. When something vibrates, it pushes and pulls on nearby particles, passing the vibration along until it reaches your ear."],
  ["how fast does sound travel | what is the speed of sound | speed of sound in air", "In air at room temperature, sound travels at about **343 meters per second**, or roughly 1,235 km/h. It moves faster in water (about 1,500 m/s) and even faster in solids like steel."],
  ["can sound travel in space | is space silent | why is there no sound in space", "No, sound can't travel through the vacuum of space because there are no air particles to carry the vibrations. That's why space is silent, and astronauts use radios to talk to each other."],
  ["what is a sonic boom | why do jets make a boom | what happens when you break the sound barrier", "A sonic boom is a loud bang caused when something travels faster than sound. The sound waves it makes pile up into a shock wave that sweeps across the ground, sounding like thunder."],
  ["what is an echo | how do echoes work | why do echoes happen", "An echo is a sound you hear again after it bounces off a hard surface, like a cliff or a large wall. You notice it when the reflecting surface is far enough away that the bounce arrives noticeably later than the original sound."],
  ["what is pitch | why do some sounds sound high and others low | what is frequency of sound", "Pitch is how high or low a sound seems, and it depends on frequency: how many vibrations happen each second. Fast vibrations make high-pitched sounds, slow ones make low sounds. Frequency is measured in **hertz (Hz)**."],
  ["what can humans hear | what is the range of human hearing | what frequencies can we hear", "Humans can usually hear sounds from about **20 Hz to 20,000 Hz**. The upper limit drops as we get older. Dogs, bats and dolphins can hear much higher pitches than we can."],
  ["what is ultrasound | how does ultrasound work", "Ultrasound is sound with a frequency too high for humans to hear, above about 20,000 Hz. Doctors use ultrasound echoes to make pictures of the inside of the body, and bats and dolphins use it to find their way around."],
  ["what is a decibel | how is loudness measured", "Loudness is measured in **decibels (dB)**. A whisper is about 30 dB, normal talking about 60 dB, and a rock concert can be over 100 dB. Long exposure to sounds above about 85 dB can damage your hearing."],
  ["what is electricity | how does electricity work | define electricity", "Electricity is the flow of tiny charged particles called **electrons**. When electrons move through a wire, they carry energy that can power lights, phones and motors. This flow is called an electric current."],
  ["what is a circuit | how does an electric circuit work | what is a closed circuit", "A circuit is a complete loop that electricity can flow around, usually made of a power source (like a battery), wires and a device (like a bulb). If the loop is broken, such as by flipping a switch off, the current stops."],
  ["what is the difference between voltage and current | what is voltage | what is current", "Voltage is the 'push' that drives electrons through a circuit, measured in volts. Current is how much electric charge actually flows per second, measured in amps. A common comparison is water in a pipe: voltage is the pressure and current is how much water flows."],
  ["what is resistance | what is ohm's law | what is an ohm", "Resistance is how much a material opposes the flow of electric current, measured in **ohms (Ω)**. Ohm's law links them: voltage = current × resistance (V = I × R)."],
  ["what is a conductor | what is an insulator | conductors vs insulators", "A conductor lets electricity flow easily; most metals, like copper, are good conductors. An insulator blocks the flow; rubber, plastic, glass and wood are good insulators. That's why wires are copper wrapped in plastic."],
  ["how does a battery work | what is inside a battery | how do batteries make electricity", "A battery stores chemical energy and turns it into electrical energy. Chemical reactions inside push electrons out of one end (the negative terminal), through your device, and back into the other end (the positive terminal)."],
  ["what is static electricity | why do i get shocked when i touch a door handle | why does my hair stand up", "Static electricity is a buildup of electric charge on a surface. Rubbing things together, like socks on carpet, can move electrons from one material to another. When you touch metal, the extra charge jumps away in a tiny spark: zap!"],
  ["what is ac and dc | difference between ac and dc | alternating current vs direct current", "Direct current (DC) flows in one direction, like from a battery. Alternating current (AC) rapidly switches direction back and forth and is what comes out of wall sockets, because it's easier to send long distances through power lines."],
  ["how is electricity made | how do power plants make electricity | where does electricity come from", "Most electricity comes from generators, which spin magnets near coils of wire to make current flow. Something has to spin them: steam from burning fuel or nuclear reactions, flowing water, or wind. Solar panels are different; they turn sunlight directly into electricity."],
  ["who invented electricity | who discovered electricity", "Electricity is a natural force, so no one invented it. Many scientists helped us understand it, including Benjamin Franklin (lightning experiments), Alessandro Volta (the first battery), Michael Faraday (generators and motors) and Thomas Edison and Nikola Tesla (power systems)."],
  ["what is a magnet | how do magnets work | why do magnets attract", "A magnet is an object that creates a magnetic field, which can pull on iron and certain other metals. Magnets have a north and south pole: opposite poles attract and like poles repel. Magnetism comes from the way electrons spin and move inside the material."],
  ["why do magnets stick to the fridge | what metals do magnets attract | do magnets stick to all metals", "Magnets strongly attract iron, nickel and cobalt, and steel because it contains iron. Fridge doors are usually made of steel, so magnets stick. Metals like aluminum, copper, gold and silver are not attracted in any noticeable way."],
  ["how does a compass work | why does a compass point north", "A compass needle is a small magnet that lines up with Earth's magnetic field. Earth acts like a giant magnet, so the needle's north end points toward Earth's magnetic north pole, which is near the geographic North Pole."],
  ["does earth have a magnetic field | why does earth have a magnetic field | what is the magnetosphere", "Yes. Earth's magnetic field is created by molten iron swirling in its outer core. This field, called the magnetosphere, protects us from much of the harmful charged particles streaming from the Sun."],
  ["what is an electromagnet | how does an electromagnet work", "An electromagnet is a magnet made by running electric current through a coil of wire, often wrapped around iron. Turn the current off and the magnetism disappears. They're used in motors, speakers, doorbells and scrapyard cranes."],
  ["what is an atom | what are atoms made of | define atom", "An atom is the smallest unit of a chemical element. It has a tiny, dense center called the **nucleus**, made of protons and neutrons, surrounded by a cloud of electrons. Everything around you is made of atoms."],
  ["what are protons neutrons and electrons | parts of an atom | what is a proton", "Atoms have three main particles:\n- **Protons**: positive charge, in the nucleus\n- **Neutrons**: no charge, in the nucleus\n- **Electrons**: negative charge, moving around the nucleus\nThe number of protons decides which element an atom is."],
  ["how small is an atom | how big is an atom | how many atoms are in a human", "Atoms are incredibly small: about 0.1 to 0.5 nanometers across, so hundreds of thousands would fit across the width of a human hair. A human body contains roughly 7 billion billion billion (7 × 10²⁷) atoms."],
  ["what is a molecule | difference between atom and molecule", "A molecule is two or more atoms bonded together. For example, a water molecule (H₂O) is two hydrogen atoms joined to one oxygen atom. Atoms are the building blocks; molecules are what you build with them."],
  ["what are quarks | what are protons made of | what is the smallest particle", "Protons and neutrons are made of even smaller particles called **quarks**, held together by the strong force. Quarks and electrons are considered fundamental particles, meaning as far as we know they aren't made of anything smaller."],
  ["who discovered the atom | who came up with the idea of atoms", "The ancient Greek thinker Democritus first suggested matter was made of tiny uncuttable pieces he called 'atomos'. In the 1800s, John Dalton developed the modern atomic theory, and later J.J. Thomson discovered the electron and Ernest Rutherford discovered the nucleus."],
  ["what is nuclear energy | how does nuclear power work | what is nuclear fission", "Nuclear power plants use **fission**: splitting heavy atoms like uranium. Each split releases a lot of heat, which boils water into steam to spin turbines and make electricity. A small amount of fuel produces a huge amount of energy."],
  ["what is nuclear fusion | fission vs fusion | difference between fusion and fission", "Fusion is when light atoms, like hydrogen, smash together to form a heavier atom, releasing enormous energy. It powers the Sun and stars. Fission is the opposite: splitting heavy atoms apart, which is what nuclear power plants use."],
  ["what is radioactivity | what does radioactive mean | what is radiation", "Radioactive materials have unstable atoms that break down over time, giving off energy as radiation. Some radiation can be harmful in large amounts, but it's also used in medicine, smoke detectors and dating ancient objects."],
  ["what is e equals mc squared | what does e=mc2 mean | explain e = mc2", "E = mc² is Einstein's famous equation showing that mass and energy are two forms of the same thing. E is energy, m is mass, and c is the speed of light. Because c² is enormous, even a tiny bit of mass holds a huge amount of energy."],
  ["what is the theory of relativity | explain relativity simply | what did einstein's relativity say", "Einstein's relativity has two parts. **Special relativity** (1905) says the speed of light is the same for everyone and that time and space can stretch depending on how fast you move. **General relativity** (1915) explains gravity as mass curving space and time."],
  ["is time travel possible | can we time travel | can you travel back in time", "Traveling into the future is possible in a small way: relativity shows time passes slower for very fast-moving objects or in strong gravity, so astronauts age a tiny bit differently. Traveling back in time has never been shown to be possible and may be forbidden by physics."],
  ["what is time dilation | does time slow down near the speed of light", "Time dilation means time passes at different rates for different observers. Clocks moving very fast or sitting in strong gravity tick more slowly. GPS satellites actually have to correct for this to give accurate locations."],
  ["what is quantum physics | what is quantum mechanics | explain quantum physics simply", "Quantum physics is the science of the very small, like atoms and photons. At that scale, things behave strangely: particles can act like waves, exist in a mix of states until measured, and be linked across distances. It underlies lasers, transistors and computer chips."],
  ["what is heat | what is the difference between heat and temperature | heat vs temperature", "Temperature measures how fast the particles in something are jiggling on average. Heat is the energy that flows from a hotter object to a colder one. A bathtub of warm water holds more heat energy than a cup of boiling water, even though the cup is hotter."],
  ["what is absolute zero | what is the coldest temperature possible | how cold is absolute zero", "Absolute zero is the coldest temperature possible: **-273.15 °C** (0 kelvin, or -459.67 °F). At that point particles would have the minimum possible motion. Scientists have gotten extremely close but can never quite reach it."],
  ["how does heat travel | what are conduction convection and radiation | ways heat moves", "Heat moves in three ways:\n- **Conduction**: through direct touch, like a hot pan handle\n- **Convection**: through moving liquids or gases, like warm air rising\n- **Radiation**: as waves through space, like sunlight warming your face"],
  ["what is the boiling point of water | at what temperature does water boil", "At sea level, pure water boils at **100 °C (212 °F)**. At high altitudes, where air pressure is lower, water boils at a lower temperature, which is why cooking pasta takes longer in the mountains."],
  ["what is the freezing point of water | at what temperature does water freeze", "Pure water freezes at **0 °C (32 °F)**. Adding salt lowers the freezing point, which is why salt is spread on icy roads."],
  ["how do you convert celsius to fahrenheit | celsius to fahrenheit formula | fahrenheit to celsius", "To go from Celsius to Fahrenheit, multiply by 9/5 and add 32 (°F = °C × 1.8 + 32). To go back, subtract 32 and multiply by 5/9. For example, 20 °C is 68 °F."],
  ["what is a simple machine | types of simple machines | list the simple machines", "Simple machines make work easier by changing the size or direction of a force. The six classic ones are:\n- Lever\n- Wheel and axle\n- Pulley\n- Inclined plane (ramp)\n- Wedge\n- Screw"],
  ["what is work in physics | how do you calculate work", "In physics, work is done when a force moves something. You calculate it as force × distance moved in the direction of the force. It's measured in joules, the same unit as energy."],
  ["what is power in physics | what is a watt", "Power is how fast energy is used or work is done. It's measured in **watts**: one watt is one joule per second. A 10-watt LED bulb uses 10 joules of energy every second."],
  ["what is chemistry | what do chemists study | define chemistry", "Chemistry is the science of matter: what things are made of, how they behave, and how they change when they react with each other. It explains everything from cooking and rusting to medicines and fireworks."],
  ["what is an element | what is a chemical element | define element", "An element is a pure substance made of only one kind of atom, such as oxygen, gold or carbon. Each element has a unique number of protons in its atoms. There are 118 known elements."],
  ["how many elements are there | how many elements are on the periodic table", "There are **118** confirmed elements on the periodic table. About 90 of them occur naturally on Earth, and the rest have been made in laboratories."],
  ["what is the periodic table | who made the periodic table | how is the periodic table organized", "The periodic table is a chart that arranges all the elements by their atomic number (number of protons). Elements in the same column behave in similar ways. Russian chemist **Dmitri Mendeleev** created the first widely accepted version in 1869."],
  ["what is the most common element in the universe | most abundant element", "**Hydrogen** is the most common element in the universe, making up about three-quarters of all normal matter. Helium is second. Together they were formed mostly in the Big Bang."],
  ["what is the most common element on earth | what is earth made of mostly", "By mass, Earth as a whole is mostly iron (much of it in the core), followed by oxygen, silicon and magnesium. In Earth's crust, oxygen is the most common element, followed by silicon."],
  ["what is the most common gas in the air | what is air made of | what gases are in the atmosphere", "Air is about **78% nitrogen** and **21% oxygen**, with small amounts of argon, carbon dioxide, water vapor and other gases. Even though we need oxygen to breathe, most of each breath is actually nitrogen."],
  ["what is the lightest element | what is the first element", "**Hydrogen** is the lightest and simplest element, with just one proton and one electron. It's number 1 on the periodic table."],
  ["what is the heaviest natural element | what is the densest element", "The densest elements are **osmium** and **iridium**, which are about twice as dense as lead. Uranium is the heaviest element (by atomic mass) found in meaningful amounts in nature."],
  ["what is a compound | difference between element and compound", "A compound is a substance made of two or more different elements chemically bonded together, like water (hydrogen and oxygen) or table salt (sodium and chlorine). Compounds often have very different properties from the elements that make them."],
  ["what is a mixture | difference between mixture and compound", "A mixture is two or more substances mixed together but not chemically joined, like salad, sand and water, or air. You can usually separate a mixture by physical means, such as filtering or evaporating. A compound needs a chemical reaction to separate."],
  ["what is the chemical formula for water | what is h2o | why is water called h2o", "Water's chemical formula is **H₂O**, meaning each molecule has two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom."],
  ["what is the chemical symbol for gold | why is gold au", "Gold's chemical symbol is **Au**, from its Latin name 'aurum'. Silver is **Ag** (argentum), iron is **Fe** (ferrum) and lead is **Pb** (plumbum), which is why some symbols don't match their English names."],
  ["what is co2 | what is carbon dioxide", "CO₂ is carbon dioxide, a gas made of one carbon atom and two oxygen atoms. Animals breathe it out, plants take it in for photosynthesis, and burning fuels releases it. It's also a greenhouse gas that traps heat in the atmosphere."],
  ["what is oxygen | why do we need oxygen", "Oxygen is a colorless gas that makes up about 21% of the air. Our cells use it to release energy from food in a process called respiration. It's also needed for fire to burn."],
  ["what is carbon | why is carbon important", "Carbon is element number 6 and the backbone of all known life. Its atoms can bond in chains and rings, forming millions of different molecules like proteins, sugars and DNA. Diamond, graphite (pencil lead) and coal are all forms of carbon."],
  ["are diamonds made of carbon | how are diamonds formed | what is a diamond made of", "Yes, diamonds are pure carbon. They form deep underground, roughly 150 km down, where intense heat and pressure squeeze carbon atoms into a very strong crystal structure. Volcanic eruptions carry them closer to the surface."],
  ["what is helium | why does helium make your voice squeaky | why do helium balloons float", "Helium is a very light, unreactive gas. Balloons filled with it float because helium is less dense than air. It makes voices sound higher because sound travels faster through helium, changing how your voice resonates. Breathing it can be dangerous, so it's best avoided."],
  ["what are the states of matter | how many states of matter are there | solid liquid gas", "The three everyday states of matter are **solid**, **liquid** and **gas**. A fourth common state is **plasma**, found in stars and lightning. Solids keep their shape, liquids take the shape of their container, and gases spread out to fill any space."],
  ["what is plasma | what is the fourth state of matter", "Plasma is a super-hot state of matter where atoms have lost some of their electrons, creating a glowing mix of charged particles. Stars, lightning, neon signs and auroras are all made of plasma. It's actually the most common state of visible matter in the universe."],
  ["what is melting | what is evaporation | what is condensation", "These are changes of state:\n- **Melting**: solid to liquid\n- **Freezing**: liquid to solid\n- **Evaporation/boiling**: liquid to gas\n- **Condensation**: gas to liquid\n- **Sublimation**: solid straight to gas, like dry ice"],
  ["why does ice float | why does ice float on water | is ice lighter than water", "Ice floats because it's about 9% less dense than liquid water. When water freezes, its molecules lock into an open crystal pattern that takes up more space. This is unusual; most substances are denser as solids. It also helps fish survive winter under frozen lakes."],
  ["why is water important | why is water special | why do living things need water", "Water is essential for all known life. It dissolves more substances than almost any other liquid, carries nutrients around living things, helps control temperature, and is the medium for most chemical reactions in cells. Your body is roughly 60% water."],
  ["what is dry ice | is dry ice frozen water", "Dry ice is frozen carbon dioxide, not frozen water. It's extremely cold, about -78.5 °C, and turns straight from solid to gas without melting, which creates spooky fog. Never touch it with bare skin."],
  ["what is an acid | what is a base | acids and bases", "Acids are substances that release hydrogen ions in water; they taste sour, like lemon juice and vinegar. Bases (alkalis) do the opposite; they feel slippery, like soap. When an acid and a base mix, they neutralize each other."],
  ["what is the ph scale | what does ph mean | what is ph", "The pH scale measures how acidic or basic a solution is, from 0 to 14. Pure water is neutral at 7. Below 7 is acidic (lemon juice is about 2) and above 7 is basic (soap is around 9 to 10)."],
  ["what is a chemical reaction | examples of chemical reactions", "A chemical reaction is when substances change into new substances by rearranging their atoms. Signs include color changes, gas bubbles, heat, light or a new solid forming. Burning wood, baking a cake and iron rusting are all chemical reactions."],
  ["what is the difference between a physical and chemical change | physical vs chemical change", "A physical change alters how something looks or its state but not what it's made of, like melting ice or cutting paper. A chemical change makes a new substance, like burning paper or cooking an egg, and is usually hard to reverse."],
  ["why does iron rust | what is rust | how does rust form", "Rust forms when iron reacts with oxygen and water to make iron oxide, a flaky reddish-brown substance. Salt speeds it up, which is why cars rust faster where roads are salted. Paint, oil or zinc coatings help prevent it."],
  ["what is fire | how does fire work | what is fire made of", "Fire is a chemical reaction called combustion, where fuel reacts quickly with oxygen and releases heat and light. A fire needs three things, called the fire triangle: **fuel**, **oxygen** and **heat**. Remove any one and it goes out."],
  ["why does baking soda and vinegar fizz | baking soda and vinegar reaction", "Vinegar is an acid and baking soda is a base. When they mix, they react to produce carbon dioxide gas, which makes all those bubbles. It's a classic, safe science volcano experiment."],
  ["what is salt made of | what is table salt", "Table salt is **sodium chloride (NaCl)**. Interestingly, sodium is a reactive metal and chlorine is a toxic gas, but bonded together they make the harmless salt we sprinkle on food."],
  ["what is a solution | what does dissolve mean | why does sugar dissolve in water", "A solution forms when one substance (the solute) spreads evenly through another (the solvent). When sugar dissolves, water molecules pull the sugar molecules apart and surround them, so they spread out invisibly. Warm water usually dissolves sugar faster."],
  ["what is a catalyst | what do catalysts do", "A catalyst speeds up a chemical reaction without being used up itself. Car catalytic converters use metals like platinum to clean exhaust, and enzymes in your body are natural catalysts that help digest food."],
  ["what is an isotope | what are isotopes", "Isotopes are versions of the same element with different numbers of neutrons. For example, carbon-12 and carbon-14 are both carbon, but carbon-14 has two extra neutrons and is slightly radioactive. Scientists use carbon-14 to date ancient once-living things."],
  ["what is carbon dating | how does carbon dating work | how do scientists know how old things are", "Living things take in carbon, including a little radioactive carbon-14. After they die, the carbon-14 slowly decays at a known rate, halving about every 5,730 years. By measuring how much is left, scientists can estimate ages up to about 50,000 years."],
  ["what is biology | what do biologists study | define biology", "Biology is the science of living things: how they're built, how they work, how they grow and reproduce, and how they evolved and interact with their environment. It ranges from tiny cells to entire ecosystems."],
  ["what is a cell | what are cells | define cell", "A cell is the smallest unit of life. Every living thing is made of one or more cells. Bacteria are just a single cell, while your body has around 30 trillion of them working together."],
  ["what are the parts of a cell | what is in a cell | what are organelles", "Key parts of a cell include:\n- **Cell membrane**: the outer boundary\n- **Nucleus**: holds DNA and controls the cell\n- **Cytoplasm**: the jelly-like inside\n- **Mitochondria**: release energy from food\n- **Ribosomes**: build proteins\nPlant cells also have a cell wall and chloroplasts."],
  ["what is the mitochondria | what does the mitochondria do | mitochondria powerhouse of the cell", "Mitochondria are often called the **powerhouse of the cell**. They use oxygen to break down sugar and release energy in a form the cell can use, called ATP. Muscle cells contain lots of them because they need so much energy."],
  ["difference between plant and animal cells | plant cell vs animal cell", "Plant cells have three things animal cells don't: a rigid **cell wall**, **chloroplasts** for photosynthesis, and usually one large **vacuole** for storing water. Animal cells are often rounder and more flexible."],
  ["what is dna | what does dna stand for | what does dna do", "DNA stands for **deoxyribonucleic acid**. It's the molecule that carries the instructions for building and running a living thing. It's shaped like a twisted ladder called a **double helix**, and it's passed from parents to offspring."],
  ["who discovered the structure of dna | who found the double helix", "James Watson and Francis Crick described DNA's double-helix structure in 1953. Their work relied heavily on X-ray images made by **Rosalind Franklin** and Maurice Wilkins. Franklin's 'Photo 51' was key evidence."],
  ["what is a gene | what are genes | difference between dna and genes", "A gene is a section of DNA that holds the instructions for making a particular protein or trait, like part of your eye color. Your DNA contains about 20,000 genes. Think of DNA as a cookbook and genes as individual recipes."],
  ["what are chromosomes | how many chromosomes do humans have", "Chromosomes are tightly packed bundles of DNA found in the nucleus of cells. Humans usually have **46 chromosomes** in 23 pairs, getting one of each pair from each parent."],
  ["how much dna do humans share with chimpanzees | are we related to chimps | how similar are humans and chimps", "Humans and chimpanzees share roughly **98 to 99%** of their DNA sequences. We didn't evolve from chimps, though; we share a common ancestor that lived around 6 to 7 million years ago."],
  ["what is genetics | what is heredity | why do kids look like their parents", "Genetics is the study of genes and how traits are passed from parents to children, which is called heredity. You get half your DNA from each parent, so you inherit a unique mix of their features."],
  ["who is the father of genetics | who was gregor mendel", "**Gregor Mendel** was a 19th-century monk who experimented with pea plants and discovered the basic rules of how traits are inherited. He's known as the father of genetics, though his work wasn't widely recognized until after his death."],
  ["what is photosynthesis | how does photosynthesis work | how do plants make food", "Photosynthesis is how plants make their own food. Using energy from sunlight, they combine carbon dioxide from the air and water from the soil to make sugar (glucose), releasing oxygen as a bonus. It happens in chloroplasts, which contain green chlorophyll."],
  ["what is the equation for photosynthesis | photosynthesis formula", "The simple word equation is: carbon dioxide + water + light energy → glucose + oxygen. In symbols: 6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂."],
  ["why are plants green | what is chlorophyll", "Plants are green because of **chlorophyll**, the pigment that captures sunlight for photosynthesis. Chlorophyll absorbs mostly red and blue light and reflects green light, which is what we see."],
  ["why do leaves change color in fall | why do leaves turn red and yellow in autumn", "In autumn, trees stop making chlorophyll as days get shorter and cooler. As the green fades, yellow and orange pigments that were there all along show through. Some trees also make new red pigments before their leaves drop."],
  ["what is cellular respiration | what is respiration in biology", "Cellular respiration is how cells release energy from food. They combine glucose with oxygen to make energy (ATP), carbon dioxide and water. It's basically photosynthesis in reverse, and it happens in the mitochondria."],
  ["what is evolution | what is the theory of evolution | how does evolution work", "Evolution is the process by which living things change over many generations. Individuals with traits that help them survive and reproduce pass those traits on more often, so populations gradually change. Over long periods, this can lead to new species."],
  ["what is natural selection | define natural selection | survival of the fittest", "Natural selection is the main way evolution happens. Living things vary, and those with traits better suited to their environment tend to survive and have more offspring, passing those traits on. 'Fittest' means best suited, not necessarily strongest."],
  ["did humans evolve from monkeys | do humans come from monkeys", "Not exactly. Humans didn't evolve from today's monkeys or apes. Instead, humans and other apes share common ancestors that lived millions of years ago, like cousins sharing great-great-grandparents."],
  ["how did life begin on earth | how did life start | when did life start on earth", "Scientists think life began on Earth at least **3.5 billion years ago**, possibly earlier. Exactly how is still being researched, but leading ideas involve simple molecules forming in water, perhaps near deep-sea hot vents, and eventually learning to copy themselves."],
  ["what is a species | how many species are there on earth", "A species is a group of living things that can breed together and have fertile offspring. Scientists have named around 2 million species, but estimates suggest there may be millions more still undiscovered, especially insects and microbes."],
  ["what is the difference between a virus and bacteria | virus vs bacteria | are viruses and bacteria the same", "Bacteria are tiny living single cells that can survive on their own; many are helpful, but some cause illness. Viruses are much smaller and can only reproduce by invading a living cell. Antibiotics can treat bacterial infections but don't work on viruses."],
  ["what are bacteria | are all bacteria bad | are bacteria good", "Bacteria are single-celled microorganisms found almost everywhere. Most are harmless or helpful: bacteria in your gut help digest food, and others are used to make yogurt and cheese. Only a small fraction cause disease."],
  ["what is a virus | are viruses alive | how do viruses work", "A virus is a tiny bundle of genetic material in a protein coat. It can't eat, grow or reproduce on its own; it has to hijack a living cell to make copies of itself. Because of that, scientists debate whether viruses count as alive."],
  ["how do vaccines work | what does a vaccine do | how do vaccinations protect you", "Vaccines train your immune system to recognize a germ without you having to get sick first. They contain a harmless piece or weakened version of the germ, or instructions to make a piece of it. Your body then makes antibodies and memory cells ready to fight the real thing."],
  ["who invented vaccines | who made the first vaccine", "**Edward Jenner** created the first vaccine in 1796, using material from cowpox to protect people against smallpox. The word 'vaccine' comes from 'vacca', Latin for cow. Smallpox was later completely wiped out worldwide in 1980."],
  ["what are antibiotics | who discovered penicillin", "Antibiotics are medicines that kill bacteria or stop them from growing. The first widely used one, **penicillin**, was discovered by Alexander Fleming in 1928 when he noticed mold killing bacteria in a dish. Antibiotics don't work against viruses like colds or flu."],
  ["what is the immune system | how does the immune system work | how does your body fight germs", "The immune system is your body's defense force against germs. Skin acts as a barrier, white blood cells attack invaders, and antibodies tag germs for destruction. It also remembers past infections so it can respond faster next time."],
  ["what are germs | what are microorganisms | what are microbes", "Germs, or microbes, are living things too small to see without a microscope, including bacteria, viruses, fungi and protists. Most are harmless, but some cause disease. Washing your hands with soap is one of the best ways to stop harmful ones spreading."],
  ["why do we need to wash our hands | does soap kill germs | how does soap work", "Soap molecules have one end that sticks to water and another that sticks to oils and grease. They break apart the oily layer that holds dirt and many germs, letting water rinse them away. Scrubbing for about 20 seconds works best."],
  ["what is a fungus | are mushrooms plants | what are fungi", "Fungi are their own kingdom of life, separate from plants and animals. They include mushrooms, molds and yeasts. Unlike plants, they can't make food from sunlight; they absorb nutrients from their surroundings, often breaking down dead material."],
  ["what is the food chain | what is a food web", "A food chain shows who eats whom in nature, like grass → rabbit → fox. Plants are producers, animals that eat them are consumers, and decomposers like fungi break down dead things. A food web connects many food chains together."],
  ["what is an ecosystem | define ecosystem", "An ecosystem is a community of living things interacting with each other and their environment, like a forest, pond or coral reef. Everything is connected, so a change to one part can affect the whole system."],
  ["what is a mammal | what makes an animal a mammal", "Mammals are warm-blooded animals with hair or fur that feed their babies milk. Most give birth to live young. Humans, dogs, whales and bats are all mammals; even platypuses, which lay eggs, are mammals."],
  ["is a whale a fish | why is a whale a mammal | is a dolphin a fish", "No, whales and dolphins are mammals, not fish. They breathe air through lungs, are warm-blooded, give birth to live young and feed them milk. Fish breathe through gills."],
  ["what is the difference between reptiles and amphibians | reptiles vs amphibians", "Reptiles, like snakes and lizards, have dry, scaly skin and lay eggs with tough shells on land. Amphibians, like frogs and salamanders, have moist skin, usually lay soft eggs in water, and often start life in water before moving onto land."],
  ["what is metamorphosis | how does a caterpillar become a butterfly", "Metamorphosis is a dramatic change in body form as an animal grows. A caterpillar forms a chrysalis, and inside, its body breaks down and reorganizes into a butterfly. Frogs also go through metamorphosis, from tadpole to adult."],
  ["what is the biggest animal ever | what is the largest animal | how big is a blue whale", "The **blue whale** is the largest animal known to have ever lived, bigger than any dinosaur. It can grow to about 30 meters long and weigh around 150 tonnes or more. Its heart alone is roughly the size of a small car."],
  ["what was the biggest dinosaur | largest dinosaur ever", "The biggest dinosaurs were long-necked sauropods like **Argentinosaurus** and Patagotitan, which may have reached 30 to 35 meters long and weighed 60 to 70 tonnes or more. Exact sizes are estimates since only partial skeletons have been found."],
  ["what are dinosaurs | when did dinosaurs live | how long ago did dinosaurs live", "Dinosaurs were a group of reptiles that ruled the land for about 165 million years. They first appeared around **230 million years ago** and the non-bird dinosaurs died out about **66 million years ago**."],
  ["how did the dinosaurs die | what killed the dinosaurs | why did dinosaurs go extinct", "Most scientists agree a giant asteroid, about 10 km wide, struck near what is now Mexico's Yucatán Peninsula around 66 million years ago. The impact caused fires, tsunamis and a long 'winter' of dust that blocked sunlight. Huge volcanic eruptions in India may have added to the disaster."],
  ["are birds dinosaurs | did dinosaurs turn into birds", "Yes! Birds evolved from small, feathered meat-eating dinosaurs, so scientists consider birds to be living dinosaurs. A chicken is more closely related to T. rex than to a crocodile."],
  ["how big was t rex | what was tyrannosaurus rex like", "Tyrannosaurus rex was one of the largest meat-eating dinosaurs, around 12 meters long and weighing about 8 tonnes. It had a bite force among the strongest of any land animal. It lived about 68 to 66 million years ago, in what is now North America."],
  ["did humans and dinosaurs live at the same time | did cavemen see dinosaurs", "No. The non-bird dinosaurs died out about 66 million years ago, while the earliest humans appeared only a few million years ago. Modern humans have been around for roughly 300,000 years."],
  ["what is a fossil | how are fossils formed", "A fossil is the preserved remains or trace of an ancient living thing, like bones, shells, footprints or leaf imprints. Most form when a creature is buried in sediment, and over millions of years minerals slowly replace it and turn it to stone."],
  ["what is a paleontologist | who studies dinosaurs", "A paleontologist is a scientist who studies ancient life through fossils, including dinosaurs, early plants and prehistoric animals. Archaeologists, by contrast, study human history through artifacts."],
  ["what is extinction | what does extinct mean", "A species is extinct when every last member has died out. Extinction has happened naturally throughout Earth's history, including five major mass extinctions. Today, human activities like habitat loss are speeding up the rate of extinction."],
  ["were pterodactyls dinosaurs | are pterosaurs dinosaurs | were swimming reptiles dinosaurs", "No. Pterosaurs (like pterodactyls) were flying reptiles closely related to dinosaurs but not dinosaurs themselves. Big sea reptiles like plesiosaurs and mosasaurs weren't dinosaurs either."],
  ["how many bones are in the human body | how many bones do adults have | how many bones does a baby have", "An adult human has **206 bones**. Babies are born with around 270 to 300 bones and cartilage pieces, but many fuse together as they grow."],
  ["what is the smallest bone in the body | smallest bone in the human body", "The smallest bone is the **stapes** (stirrup) in your middle ear. It's only about 3 millimeters long and helps carry sound vibrations to your inner ear."],
  ["what is the longest bone in the body | biggest bone in the human body | what is the femur", "The **femur**, or thigh bone, is the longest and strongest bone in the human body. It makes up roughly a quarter of a person's height."],
  ["what is the largest organ in the body | biggest organ in the human body", "Your **skin** is the largest organ, covering about 2 square meters in an adult. It protects you from germs, helps control your temperature, and lets you feel touch. The liver is the largest internal organ."],
  ["what does the heart do | how does the heart work | how does blood circulate", "The heart is a muscular pump about the size of your fist. It has four chambers and pushes blood through your body, carrying oxygen and nutrients to cells and taking away waste. It beats about 100,000 times a day."],
  ["what is a normal heart rate | how fast does the heart beat | how many times does your heart beat", "A normal resting heart rate for adults is about **60 to 100 beats per minute**. Kids' hearts usually beat faster, and very fit athletes may have resting rates below 60. Exercise, excitement and fear all speed it up."],
  ["what is blood made of | what is in blood | what are red and white blood cells", "Blood is made of:\n- **Plasma**: the yellowish liquid part\n- **Red blood cells**: carry oxygen\n- **White blood cells**: fight infections\n- **Platelets**: help blood clot when you get a cut"],
  ["why is blood red | is blood blue inside your body | why do veins look blue", "Blood is always red because of **hemoglobin**, an iron-containing protein that carries oxygen. Oxygen-rich blood is bright red and oxygen-poor blood is darker red, but never blue. Veins only look blue because of how light passes through skin."],
  ["how much blood is in the human body | how many liters of blood do we have", "An average adult has about **5 liters** of blood, roughly 7 to 8% of their body weight. Children have less, depending on their size."],
  ["what are the blood types | what blood type is universal donor | what are blood groups", "The main blood types are **A, B, AB and O**, each either positive or negative. O negative is known as the universal donor for red blood cells, and AB positive is the universal recipient."],
  ["what is the difference between arteries and veins | arteries vs veins | what are capillaries", "Arteries carry blood away from the heart, usually rich in oxygen. Veins carry blood back to the heart. Tiny capillaries connect them, letting oxygen and nutrients pass into cells."],
  ["what do the lungs do | how do lungs work | how do we breathe", "Your lungs take in oxygen and get rid of carbon dioxide. When you breathe in, a muscle called the **diaphragm** moves down, pulling air in. Inside, millions of tiny air sacs called alveoli pass oxygen into your blood."],
  ["how does the brain work | what does the brain do | what is the brain made of", "Your brain is your body's control center. It's made of about **86 billion neurons** that send electrical and chemical signals to each other. It controls your thoughts, memories, movement, senses and automatic actions like breathing."],
  ["what are the parts of the brain | what does the cerebellum do | what does the cerebrum do", "The brain has three main parts:\n- **Cerebrum**: the largest part; thinking, memory, senses and movement\n- **Cerebellum**: balance and coordination\n- **Brainstem**: automatic functions like breathing and heartbeat"],
  ["do we only use 10 percent of our brain | is the 10% brain myth true", "No, that's a myth. Brain scans show we use virtually all of our brain, just not every part at the same moment. Different areas handle different tasks, and even during sleep many regions stay active."],
  ["what is the nervous system | how do nerves work | what are neurons", "The nervous system is your body's messaging network, made of the brain, spinal cord and nerves. Nerve cells called neurons pass signals along at up to about 120 meters per second, letting you sense the world and move."],
  ["what are the five senses | what are our senses | list the 5 senses", "The five classic senses are **sight**, **hearing**, **smell**, **taste** and **touch**. Scientists also count others, like balance, body position, temperature and pain."],
  ["how do eyes work | how do we see | how does the eye work", "Light enters your eye through the pupil and is focused by the lens onto the **retina** at the back. The retina has cells called rods (for dim light) and cones (for color) that turn light into signals, which travel along the optic nerve to your brain."],
  ["how do ears work | how do we hear | how does hearing work", "Sound waves enter your ear canal and make your eardrum vibrate. Three tiny bones pass the vibrations to the cochlea, a snail-shaped organ filled with fluid and tiny hair cells that turn them into nerve signals for your brain."],
  ["how does taste work | what are the five tastes | what is umami", "Your tongue has thousands of taste buds that detect five basic tastes: **sweet, salty, sour, bitter and umami** (savory). Much of what we call 'flavor' actually comes from smell, which is why food tastes bland when you have a cold."],
  ["how does smell work | how does the nose smell things", "When you breathe in, tiny odor molecules reach special receptor cells high inside your nose. These send signals to your brain, which can tell apart a huge number of different smells. Smell is also closely linked to memory."],
  ["what does the stomach do | how does digestion work | what is the digestive system", "Digestion breaks food into nutrients your body can use. It starts in your mouth with chewing and saliva, then the stomach churns food with acid. The small intestine absorbs most nutrients, and the large intestine absorbs water before waste leaves the body."],
  ["how long are your intestines | how long is the small intestine", "The small intestine is about **6 meters** long in adults, and the large intestine adds about 1.5 meters. They're neatly coiled up to fit inside your belly."],
  ["what does the liver do | what is the liver for", "The liver is a hard-working organ that filters toxins from blood, makes bile to help digest fat, stores energy and vitamins, and helps blood clot. It can even regrow if part of it is removed."],
  ["what do the kidneys do | what are kidneys for", "Your two kidneys filter your blood to remove waste and extra water, which leave the body as urine. They also help control blood pressure and keep the right balance of salts and minerals."],
  ["what are muscles | how many muscles are in the body | how do muscles work", "Muscles are tissues that contract (shorten) to move your body. Humans have over **600 muscles**. They work in pairs: one pulls a bone one way, and its partner pulls it back."],
  ["what is the strongest muscle in the body | strongest muscle", "It depends how you measure. The **masseter** (jaw muscle) can bite with the most force for its size, the **gluteus maximus** is the largest muscle, and the heart is the hardest-working, beating nonstop for a lifetime."],
  ["what are teeth made of | why do we lose baby teeth | how many teeth do humans have", "Teeth are covered in **enamel**, the hardest substance in the human body. Kids have 20 baby teeth that fall out to make room for adult teeth, which number 32 including wisdom teeth."],
  ["what causes cavities | why do we get cavities | why should i brush my teeth", "Cavities form when bacteria in your mouth feed on sugar and make acid that slowly eats away at tooth enamel. Brushing twice a day with fluoride toothpaste, flossing and cutting down on sugary snacks help keep teeth healthy."],
  ["what is skin made of | why do we have skin | what are the layers of skin", "Skin has three main layers: the outer **epidermis**, the **dermis** with blood vessels, nerves and hair roots, and a deeper fatty layer. It protects you, senses touch, and helps control body temperature by sweating."],
  ["why do we sweat | what is sweat for", "Sweating cools you down. When sweat evaporates from your skin, it carries heat away with it. That's why you sweat more when it's hot or when you exercise."],
  ["what is the normal body temperature | what temperature is a fever", "Normal body temperature is around **37 °C (98.6 °F)**, though it varies a little from person to person and through the day. A temperature of about 38 °C (100.4 °F) or higher is usually considered a fever."],
  ["why do we need sleep | what happens when we sleep | why do we dream", "Sleep lets your body repair itself and your brain sort and store memories. During dreaming sleep, called REM sleep, the brain is very active. Scientists are still studying exactly why we dream, but it may help with memory and emotions."],
  ["why do we yawn | what causes yawning | why are yawns contagious", "Scientists aren't completely sure why we yawn. Leading ideas suggest it may help cool the brain or increase alertness. Yawns are contagious among people, and possibly linked to empathy, since seeing someone yawn often makes you yawn too."],
  ["why do we get hiccups | what causes hiccups", "Hiccups happen when your diaphragm suddenly twitches, making you gulp air. Your vocal cords snap shut, causing the 'hic' sound. Eating quickly, fizzy drinks or excitement can trigger them, and they usually go away on their own."],
  ["why do we have fingerprints | are fingerprints unique", "Fingerprints are ridges on your fingertips that likely help you grip things and sense textures. Everyone's are unique, even identical twins', which is why they're used for identification."],
  ["how many cells are in the human body | how many cells do we have", "An adult human body has about **30 trillion** human cells. You also carry a similar number of bacteria, mostly living in your gut."],
  ["what is the appendix for | do we need our appendix", "The appendix is a small pouch attached to the large intestine. People can live normally without it, but scientists think it may store helpful gut bacteria and play a small role in the immune system."],
  ["what are vitamins | why do we need vitamins", "Vitamins are nutrients your body needs in small amounts to work properly. For example, vitamin C helps heal wounds, vitamin D helps build strong bones, and vitamin A helps your eyes. Most come from a balanced diet, and your skin makes vitamin D in sunlight."],
  ["what is dna made of | what are the four bases of dna", "DNA is built from units called nucleotides. Its 'letters' are four bases: **adenine (A)**, **thymine (T)**, **guanine (G)** and **cytosine (C)**. A always pairs with T, and G pairs with C, forming the rungs of the double-helix ladder."],
  ["what is the solar system | what is in our solar system | how many planets are there", "The solar system is the Sun and everything that orbits it: **eight planets**, their moons, dwarf planets like Pluto, and countless asteroids and comets. It formed about 4.6 billion years ago from a cloud of gas and dust."],
  ["what are the planets in order | list the planets from the sun | order of the planets", "From the Sun outward:\n1. Mercury\n2. Venus\n3. Earth\n4. Mars\n5. Jupiter\n6. Saturn\n7. Uranus\n8. Neptune\nA handy memory trick: 'My Very Educated Mother Just Served Us Noodles.'"],
  ["is pluto a planet | why is pluto not a planet anymore | what happened to pluto", "In 2006, astronomers reclassified Pluto as a **dwarf planet**. It orbits the Sun and is round, but it hasn't cleared its orbit of other objects, which is one of the requirements for a full planet. It's still a fascinating world with mountains of ice."],
  ["what is the sun | what is the sun made of | is the sun a star", "Yes, the Sun is a star: a giant ball of hot plasma, mostly hydrogen and helium. In its core, nuclear fusion turns hydrogen into helium, releasing the energy that lights and warms Earth. It contains about 99.8% of the solar system's mass."],
  ["how hot is the sun | temperature of the sun", "The Sun's visible surface is about **5,500 °C**, while its core reaches around **15 million °C**. Strangely, its outer atmosphere, the corona, is over a million degrees, much hotter than the surface."],
  ["how big is the sun | how many earths fit in the sun | how big is the sun compared to earth", "The Sun is about **109 times wider** than Earth, and roughly **1.3 million Earths** could fit inside it. Even so, it's an average-sized star."],
  ["how far is the sun from earth | distance from earth to the sun", "Earth is about **150 million km** (93 million miles) from the Sun on average. This distance is called one astronomical unit (AU)."],
  ["how old is the sun | when will the sun die | what will happen to the sun", "The Sun is about **4.6 billion years old** and is roughly halfway through its life. In about 5 billion years, it will swell into a red giant, then shrink into a small, dense white dwarf. It's not big enough to explode as a supernova."],
  ["what are sunspots | what is a solar flare", "Sunspots are darker, cooler patches on the Sun caused by tangled magnetic fields. Solar flares are sudden bursts of energy from these areas. Strong ones can disturb satellites and radio signals and create bright auroras on Earth."],
  ["what is mercury like | tell me about mercury the planet | facts about mercury", "Mercury is the smallest planet and the closest to the Sun. A year there lasts just **88 Earth days**. With almost no atmosphere, it swings from about 430 °C in the day to about -180 °C at night."],
  ["what is venus like | why is venus so hot | facts about venus", "Venus is about the same size as Earth but is the **hottest planet**, at around 465 °C, because its thick carbon dioxide atmosphere traps heat. Its clouds contain sulfuric acid, and it spins backward compared to most planets."],
  ["what is the hottest planet | which planet is the hottest", "**Venus** is the hottest planet, around 465 °C, even though Mercury is closer to the Sun. Venus's thick atmosphere creates a runaway greenhouse effect that traps heat."],
  ["what is mars like | why is mars red | facts about mars", "Mars is called the Red Planet because its soil is rich in iron oxide, basically rust. It has the tallest volcano in the solar system, Olympus Mons, and signs that liquid water once flowed there. It has two small moons, Phobos and Deimos."],
  ["is there life on mars | could humans live on mars | has life been found on mars", "No life has been found on Mars so far. Robotic rovers have found evidence that it once had lakes and rivers, so scientists are searching for signs of ancient microbes. Living there would be very hard: the air is thin, mostly carbon dioxide, and it's extremely cold."],
  ["what is jupiter like | how big is jupiter | facts about jupiter", "Jupiter is the **largest planet**, so big that about 1,300 Earths could fit inside. It's a gas giant made mostly of hydrogen and helium, with no solid surface. Its Great Red Spot is a storm larger than Earth."],
  ["what is the great red spot | what is jupiter's red spot", "The Great Red Spot is a giant storm on Jupiter that has been raging for at least 150 years and possibly much longer. It's wider than Earth and has winds of several hundred km/h, though it has been slowly shrinking."],
  ["what is saturn like | what are saturn's rings made of | facts about saturn", "Saturn is a gas giant famous for its spectacular rings, which are made of billions of pieces of ice and rock, from tiny grains to boulders. Saturn is so light for its size that it's less dense than water."],
  ["what is uranus like | why does uranus spin on its side | facts about uranus", "Uranus is an ice giant with a blue-green color from methane in its atmosphere. It's tipped over on its side, probably from a huge collision long ago, so it rolls around the Sun like a ball. It's very cold, around -224 °C at its coldest."],
  ["what is neptune like | facts about neptune | how windy is neptune", "Neptune is the farthest planet from the Sun, a deep blue ice giant. It has the fastest winds in the solar system, over 2,000 km/h. One year there lasts about **165 Earth years**."],
  ["what is the coldest planet | which planet is the coldest", "**Uranus** holds the record for the coldest temperature measured in a planet's atmosphere, about -224 °C, even though Neptune is farther from the Sun. Neptune's average is similarly frigid."],
  ["which planet has the most moons | how many moons does saturn have", "Saturn and Jupiter each have well over 90 known moons, and astronomers keep discovering small new ones, so the count changes over time. Saturn has recently been ahead. Mercury and Venus have no moons at all."],
  ["which planets have rings | does jupiter have rings | do other planets have rings", "All four giant planets have rings: **Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus and Neptune**. Saturn's are by far the brightest and biggest; the others have thin, dark rings that are hard to see."],
  ["what is the biggest planet | what is the smallest planet | largest and smallest planets", "**Jupiter** is the biggest planet, about 11 times wider than Earth. **Mercury** is the smallest, only a little bigger than our Moon."],
  ["how old is the earth | when did earth form | age of the earth", "Earth is about **4.5 billion years old**. Scientists know this from dating the oldest rocks and meteorites using radioactive elements that decay at known rates."],
  ["how big is the earth | what is the circumference of the earth | how wide is earth", "Earth is about **12,700 km** wide (its diameter) and about **40,000 km** around the equator. It's the largest of the four rocky planets."],
  ["what is inside the earth | what are the layers of the earth | what is earth's core made of", "Earth has four main layers:\n- **Crust**: the thin, rocky outer layer we live on\n- **Mantle**: hot, slowly flowing rock\n- **Outer core**: liquid iron and nickel\n- **Inner core**: solid iron and nickel, about as hot as the Sun's surface"],
  ["is the earth round | is the earth flat | how do we know the earth is round", "Earth is round, a slightly squashed sphere that bulges at the equator. We know from photos taken from space, ships disappearing hull-first over the horizon, Earth's round shadow on the Moon during lunar eclipses, and people flying all the way around it."],
  ["why do we have day and night | what causes day and night", "Earth spins on its axis once about every **24 hours**. The side facing the Sun has daytime, and the side facing away has night. As Earth turns, the Sun appears to rise in the east and set in the west."],
  ["how long does it take earth to orbit the sun | how long is a year | why is a year 365 days", "Earth takes about **365.25 days** to orbit the Sun. Calendars use 365 days and add an extra day every four years, in leap years, to make up for that extra quarter day."],
  ["why do we have seasons | what causes the seasons | why is it hot in summer", "Seasons happen because Earth's axis is tilted about **23.5 degrees**. When your half of Earth tilts toward the Sun, sunlight hits more directly and days are longer, so it's summer. When it tilts away, it's winter. It's not because Earth gets closer to the Sun."],
  ["how fast is earth moving | how fast does earth spin | how fast does earth orbit the sun", "At the equator, Earth's spin carries you along at about **1,670 km/h**. Earth also races around the Sun at about **107,000 km/h**. We don't feel it because the motion is smooth and steady."],
  ["what is the moon | how was the moon formed | where did the moon come from", "The Moon is Earth's only natural satellite. Most scientists think it formed about 4.5 billion years ago when a Mars-sized object crashed into the young Earth, throwing out debris that clumped together into the Moon."],
  ["how far away is the moon | distance to the moon", "The Moon is about **384,000 km** from Earth on average. Light takes about 1.3 seconds to travel that far, and the Apollo astronauts took about three days to get there."],
  ["why does the moon change shape | what are the phases of the moon | why does the moon look different", "The Moon doesn't really change shape. As it orbits Earth over about 29.5 days, we see different amounts of its sunlit half. The phases go new moon, crescent, first quarter, gibbous, full moon, and then back again."],
  ["why do we always see the same side of the moon | what is the dark side of the moon", "The Moon spins exactly once for each trip around Earth, so the same side always faces us. This is called tidal locking. The 'far side' isn't actually dark; it gets just as much sunlight as the near side."],
  ["who was the first person on the moon | when did humans land on the moon | moon landing", "**Neil Armstrong** was the first person to walk on the Moon, on **July 20, 1969**, during NASA's Apollo 11 mission. Buzz Aldrin joined him, while Michael Collins orbited above. Twelve astronauts walked on the Moon between 1969 and 1972."],
  ["what did neil armstrong say on the moon | first words on the moon", "As he stepped onto the Moon, Neil Armstrong said: \"That's one small step for man, one giant leap for mankind.\""],
  ["does the moon have gravity | is there air on the moon | what is the moon made of", "The Moon has gravity, about one-sixth of Earth's, but almost no atmosphere, so there's no air to breathe. It's made of rock, covered in a layer of fine dust called regolith and countless craters from impacts."],
  ["what causes tides | how does the moon affect tides | why does the ocean have tides", "Tides are caused mainly by the Moon's gravity pulling on Earth's oceans, creating bulges of water on the side facing the Moon and the opposite side. As Earth rotates, most coastlines get two high tides and two low tides about every day. The Sun's gravity affects tides too."],
  ["what is a solar eclipse | how does a solar eclipse happen", "A solar eclipse happens when the Moon passes directly between the Sun and Earth, blocking the Sun's light. During a total eclipse, the sky goes dark in the daytime. Never look at the Sun directly, even during an eclipse, without proper eclipse glasses."],
  ["what is a lunar eclipse | how does a lunar eclipse happen | what is a blood moon", "A lunar eclipse happens when Earth passes between the Sun and the Moon, so Earth's shadow falls on the Moon. During a total lunar eclipse, the Moon often turns reddish, sometimes called a 'blood moon', because Earth's atmosphere bends red sunlight onto it. It's safe to watch."],
  ["what is a blue moon | what is a supermoon", "A **blue moon** usually means the second full moon in a single calendar month; it isn't actually blue. A **supermoon** is a full moon that happens when the Moon is near its closest point to Earth, so it looks slightly bigger and brighter."],
  ["what is a star | what are stars made of | how do stars shine", "A star is a giant ball of hot gas, mostly hydrogen and helium, held together by its own gravity. Deep in its core, nuclear fusion turns hydrogen into helium, releasing huge amounts of light and heat."],
  ["why do stars twinkle | why do stars sparkle at night", "Stars twinkle because their light passes through Earth's moving, turbulent atmosphere, which bends it slightly in different directions. Planets usually twinkle less because they appear as tiny disks rather than single points."],
  ["what is the closest star to earth | nearest star | what is proxima centauri", "The closest star is our **Sun**. The next nearest is **Proxima Centauri**, about 4.2 light-years away. Even traveling at the speed of our fastest spacecraft, it would take thousands of years to get there."],
  ["what is the brightest star in the sky | brightest star at night", "The brightest star in the night sky is **Sirius**, also called the Dog Star, about 8.6 light-years away. Planets like Venus and Jupiter can look even brighter, but they're not stars."],
  ["what is the north star | how do you find the north star | what is polaris", "The North Star, **Polaris**, sits almost directly above Earth's North Pole, so it barely moves in the sky and has guided travelers for centuries. Find it by following the two end stars of the Big Dipper's bowl upward."],
  ["what colors are stars | why are stars different colors | what is the hottest color of star", "A star's color shows its temperature. Red stars are the coolest, around 3,000 °C, yellow stars like the Sun are medium, and blue-white stars are the hottest, over 10,000 °C."],
  ["how are stars born | how do stars form | what is a nebula", "Stars are born inside nebulas, huge clouds of gas and dust in space. Gravity pulls clumps of gas together until the center becomes hot and dense enough for nuclear fusion to start, and a new star begins to shine."],
  ["how do stars die | what happens when a star dies | what is a white dwarf", "It depends on the star's size. Smaller stars like the Sun swell into red giants, then shrink into white dwarfs. Massive stars explode as supernovas, leaving behind a neutron star or a black hole."],
  ["what is a supernova | what happens in a supernova", "A supernova is the gigantic explosion of a massive star at the end of its life. For a short time it can outshine an entire galaxy. Supernovas spread heavy elements like iron and gold into space, which later become part of new stars and planets."],
  ["are we made of stardust | are humans made of star stuff", "In a way, yes! Most elements in your body, like the carbon, oxygen and iron, were made inside ancient stars and scattered into space when those stars died. The hydrogen in you is even older, dating back to the Big Bang."],
  ["what is a neutron star | what is a pulsar", "A neutron star is the super-dense core left after a massive star explodes. It packs more mass than the Sun into a ball about 20 km wide, so a teaspoon of it would weigh billions of tonnes. Pulsars are spinning neutron stars that sweep beams of radiation like lighthouses."],
  ["what is a black hole | how do black holes form | what's inside a black hole", "A black hole is a region where gravity is so strong that nothing, not even light, can escape. Many form when huge stars collapse at the end of their lives. The boundary around it is called the **event horizon**, and what happens deep inside is still a mystery."],
  ["what happens if you fall into a black hole | what is spaghettification", "Near a small black hole, gravity pulls much harder on your feet than your head, stretching you out like spaghetti; scientists really call this 'spaghettification'. Once past the event horizon, there's no way back out."],
  ["is there a black hole in the middle of the milky way | what is sagittarius a star", "Yes. At the center of our galaxy is a supermassive black hole called **Sagittarius A*** with about 4 million times the mass of the Sun. Astronomers released the first image of it in 2022."],
  ["has anyone seen a black hole | first picture of a black hole", "Black holes themselves are invisible, but we can see their effects. In 2019, the Event Horizon Telescope released the first image of a black hole's shadow, surrounded by glowing gas, in the galaxy M87."],
  ["what is a galaxy | what are galaxies | how many galaxies are there", "A galaxy is a huge collection of stars, gas, dust and dark matter held together by gravity. There are likely **hundreds of billions to around two trillion** galaxies in the observable universe."],
  ["what is the milky way | how big is the milky way | how many stars are in the milky way", "The Milky Way is our home galaxy, a spiral with roughly **100 to 400 billion stars**. It's about 100,000 light-years across, and our solar system sits in one of its spiral arms, about 26,000 light-years from the center."],
  ["what is the closest galaxy | what is andromeda | will andromeda hit the milky way", "The nearest large galaxy is **Andromeda**, about 2.5 million light-years away; you can even spot it with your naked eye from a dark site. It's heading toward us and is expected to merge with the Milky Way in roughly 4 to 5 billion years."],
  ["what types of galaxies are there | what are spiral galaxies", "Galaxies come in three main shapes: **spiral** (like the Milky Way, with curved arms), **elliptical** (smooth and oval) and **irregular** (no clear shape)."],
  ["what is a light year | how far is a light year | is a light year time or distance", "A light-year is a **distance**, not a time: how far light travels in one year, about **9.5 trillion km**. Astronomers use it because distances in space are so huge."],
  ["how big is the universe | is the universe infinite | how far can we see in space", "The **observable universe** is about **93 billion light-years** across, because space has been expanding while light traveled toward us. Beyond that we can't see, and nobody knows whether the whole universe is finite or infinite."],
  ["how old is the universe | when did the universe begin | age of the universe", "The universe is about **13.8 billion years old**. Scientists worked this out by measuring how fast it's expanding and studying the faint leftover glow from the Big Bang."],
  ["what is the big bang | how did the universe start | big bang theory", "The Big Bang theory says the universe began about 13.8 billion years ago in an extremely hot, dense state and has been expanding and cooling ever since. It wasn't an explosion in space; it was space itself expanding everywhere at once."],
  ["is the universe expanding | who discovered the universe is expanding", "Yes. In the 1920s, Edwin Hubble showed that distant galaxies are moving away from us, and the farther they are, the faster they go. In 1998, astronomers discovered the expansion is actually speeding up."],
  ["what is dark matter | what is dark energy", "**Dark matter** is invisible material that doesn't give off light but whose gravity holds galaxies together. **Dark energy** is a mysterious force making the universe's expansion speed up. Together they make up about 95% of the universe, and we still don't know exactly what they are."],
  ["what is an asteroid | what is the asteroid belt", "Asteroids are rocky or metallic objects orbiting the Sun, leftovers from the solar system's formation. Most are in the **asteroid belt** between Mars and Jupiter. They range from pebble-sized to hundreds of kilometers wide."],
  ["what is a comet | what are comets made of | why do comets have tails", "Comets are 'dirty snowballs' of ice, dust and rock that orbit the Sun. When one gets close to the Sun, its ice turns to gas, forming a glowing cloud and a long tail that always points away from the Sun."],
  ["what is a meteor | what is a shooting star | meteor vs meteorite", "A shooting star is actually a **meteor**: a small piece of space rock burning up as it streaks through Earth's atmosphere. While it's in space, it's a **meteoroid**, and if any of it survives to hit the ground, it's a **meteorite**."],
  ["what is a meteor shower | when are meteor showers", "A meteor shower happens when Earth passes through a trail of dust left by a comet, causing many shooting stars in one night. Famous ones include the Perseids in August and the Geminids in December."],
  ["what is the aurora | what causes the northern lights | what are the northern lights", "The northern and southern lights (aurora borealis and australis) happen when charged particles from the Sun hit Earth's magnetic field and collide with gases high in the atmosphere, making them glow green, pink and purple. They're best seen near the poles."],
  ["what is a constellation | how many constellations are there | what is the big dipper", "A constellation is a pattern of stars that people have named, like Orion or Ursa Major. Astronomers officially recognize **88 constellations**. The Big Dipper is a well-known star pattern that's part of Ursa Major, the Great Bear."],
  ["are there other planets outside our solar system | what is an exoplanet", "Yes! Planets orbiting other stars are called **exoplanets**. Astronomers have confirmed thousands of them since the 1990s, and some orbit in their star's 'habitable zone', where liquid water could exist."],
  ["is there life on other planets | are aliens real | is there alien life", "We haven't found any evidence of life beyond Earth yet. But with so many stars and planets in the universe, many scientists think it's possible life exists somewhere, even if just microbes. Places like Mars, Europa and Enceladus are being studied as possible homes for simple life."],
  ["what is the international space station | what is the iss | how big is the iss", "The International Space Station is a large laboratory orbiting Earth, built by several countries including the US, Russia, Europe, Japan and Canada. It's about the size of a football field, and astronauts have lived aboard continuously since 2000."],
  ["how fast does the iss go | how high is the iss | how often does the iss orbit earth", "The ISS orbits about **400 km** above Earth at roughly **28,000 km/h**. It circles Earth about every 90 minutes, so astronauts see around 16 sunrises and sunsets a day."],
  ["how do astronauts eat sleep and go to the bathroom | how do astronauts sleep in space | what do astronauts eat", "Astronauts sleep in small cabins inside sleeping bags strapped to the wall so they don't float away. They eat packaged food, often adding water, and use tortillas instead of bread to avoid crumbs. Space toilets use airflow, like a vacuum, instead of gravity."],
  ["who was the first person in space | who was yuri gagarin", "**Yuri Gagarin** of the Soviet Union became the first human in space on **April 12, 1961**, orbiting Earth once aboard Vostok 1. The flight lasted about 108 minutes."],
  ["who was the first woman in space | first american in space", "**Valentina Tereshkova** of the Soviet Union became the first woman in space in 1963. Alan Shepard was the first American in space in 1961, and John Glenn was the first American to orbit Earth in 1962."],
  ["what was the first animal in space | who was laika", "Fruit flies were sent up first, in 1947. The first animal to orbit Earth was **Laika**, a Soviet dog, in 1957 aboard Sputnik 2. Sadly, she did not survive the trip, but her flight helped pave the way for human spaceflight."],
  ["what was the first satellite | what was sputnik", "**Sputnik 1**, launched by the Soviet Union on October 4, 1957, was the first artificial satellite. It was a small metal ball with antennas that beeped radio signals, and it kicked off the Space Race."],
  ["how do rockets work | how does a rocket get to space | why do rockets need so much fuel", "Rockets burn fuel to produce hot gas that blasts out of the bottom, pushing the rocket up; it's Newton's third law in action. They need enormous amounts of fuel to reach about 28,000 km/h, the speed needed to stay in orbit."],
  ["how far away is space | where does space begin | what is the karman line", "Space is commonly said to begin at the **Kármán line**, about **100 km** above sea level. That's roughly the distance of an hour's highway drive, just straight up."],
  ["what is a spacesuit for | why do astronauts wear spacesuits", "A spacesuit is like a personal spaceship. It provides oxygen and air pressure, protects against extreme hot and cold, and shields astronauts from dust and radiation. Without one, a person couldn't survive in the vacuum of space."],
  ["what is the hubble telescope | what is the james webb telescope", "The **Hubble Space Telescope**, launched in 1990, orbits Earth and has taken some of the most famous images of space. The **James Webb Space Telescope**, launched in 2021, sees in infrared light and can look back at some of the earliest galaxies."],
  ["what is the farthest spacecraft from earth | where is voyager 1", "**Voyager 1**, launched in 1977, is the most distant human-made object. It left the Sun's main bubble of influence in 2012 and is now in interstellar space, more than 20 billion km away. It carries a golden record with sounds and images of Earth."],
  ["what is a volcano | how do volcanoes work | why do volcanoes erupt", "A volcano is an opening in Earth's crust where molten rock, gas and ash escape. Underground, melted rock called **magma** collects in chambers. When pressure builds up from gas and magma, it erupts, and the magma that comes out is called **lava**."],
  ["what is the difference between magma and lava | magma vs lava | how hot is lava", "Magma is molten rock underground; once it erupts onto the surface, it's called lava. Lava is usually about **700 to 1,200 °C**, hot enough to melt many metals."],
  ["what is the biggest volcano | what is the largest volcano on earth | what is olympus mons", "On Earth, **Mauna Loa** in Hawaii is one of the largest active volcanoes by volume. The biggest volcano in the solar system is **Olympus Mons** on Mars, about 22 km high, roughly two and a half times taller than Mount Everest."],
  ["what is an earthquake | what causes earthquakes | why do earthquakes happen", "Earthquakes happen when huge slabs of Earth's crust, called tectonic plates, suddenly slip past each other. Stress builds up along faults until the rocks break or slide, sending shock waves through the ground."],
  ["how are earthquakes measured | what is the richter scale | what is the magnitude of an earthquake", "Earthquakes are measured by magnitude, most often using the moment magnitude scale, which replaced the older Richter scale. Each whole number up means about 32 times more energy released. Instruments that detect them are called **seismographs**."],
  ["what are tectonic plates | what is plate tectonics | what is continental drift", "Earth's outer shell is broken into giant pieces called tectonic plates that slowly move a few centimeters a year, about as fast as your fingernails grow. Their movement causes earthquakes, builds mountains and creates volcanoes. Over millions of years, it has moved the continents around."],
  ["what was pangaea | were the continents once joined", "**Pangaea** was a supercontinent that included almost all of Earth's land, about 300 to 200 million years ago. It slowly broke apart because of plate tectonics, forming the continents we know today."],
  ["what is a tsunami | what causes a tsunami", "A tsunami is a series of giant ocean waves, usually caused by an undersea earthquake, landslide or volcanic eruption. In deep water they're barely noticeable, but near the coast they can grow into walls of water. If the sea suddenly pulls far back, head to high ground."],
  ["how are mountains formed | how did mount everest form", "Most big mountains form when tectonic plates collide and push the crust upward. The Himalayas, including Mount Everest, formed when the Indian plate crashed into Asia, and they're still rising a few millimeters each year."],
  ["what are the three types of rocks | types of rocks | what is igneous rock", "The three main types of rock are:\n- **Igneous**: formed from cooled magma or lava (granite, basalt)\n- **Sedimentary**: layers of sand, mud and shells pressed together (sandstone, limestone)\n- **Metamorphic**: rocks changed by heat and pressure (marble, slate)"],
  ["what is the rock cycle | how do rocks change", "The rock cycle is the slow process by which rocks change from one type to another over millions of years. Rocks can be melted, cooled, worn down into sediment, pressed into new layers, or transformed by heat and pressure, again and again."],
  ["what is erosion | what is weathering", "Weathering breaks rocks into smaller pieces, through rain, ice, wind, plants or chemicals. Erosion is when those pieces are carried away by water, wind or ice. Over millions of years, erosion carved landscapes like the Grand Canyon."],
  ["what is the water cycle | how does the water cycle work | where does rain come from", "The water cycle is the constant movement of water around Earth:\n1. **Evaporation**: the Sun heats water, turning it into vapor\n2. **Condensation**: vapor cools and forms clouds\n3. **Precipitation**: water falls as rain, snow or hail\n4. **Collection**: it gathers in oceans, lakes and groundwater, and the cycle repeats"],
  ["how are clouds formed | what are clouds made of | why do clouds float", "Clouds are made of billions of tiny water droplets or ice crystals. They form when warm, moist air rises and cools, so its water vapor condenses on tiny bits of dust. The droplets are so small and light that rising air keeps them floating."],
  ["what are the types of clouds | what are cumulus clouds | what are cirrus clouds", "The main cloud types are:\n- **Cumulus**: puffy, cotton-ball clouds, usually fair weather\n- **Stratus**: flat, gray sheets that can bring drizzle\n- **Cirrus**: thin, wispy clouds high up, made of ice\n- **Cumulonimbus**: towering storm clouds that bring thunder and heavy rain"],
  ["why does it rain | how does rain form", "Rain forms when tiny water droplets in clouds bump together and merge into bigger drops. When they become too heavy for the rising air to hold up, they fall to the ground as rain."],
  ["how does snow form | why are snowflakes six sided | are all snowflakes different", "Snow forms when water vapor freezes directly onto tiny particles in very cold clouds, growing into ice crystals. Water molecules naturally lock into a six-sided pattern, so snowflakes have six arms. Because each flake takes its own path through the cloud, no two are likely to be exactly alike."],
  ["what is hail | how does hail form", "Hail is balls of ice that form inside strong thunderstorms. Powerful updrafts toss raindrops high into freezing parts of the cloud again and again, adding layers of ice until the hailstones are too heavy and fall."],
  ["what is lightning | what causes lightning | how hot is lightning", "Lightning is a giant electric spark. Inside storm clouds, bits of ice and water collide and build up electric charges until the charge jumps between clouds or to the ground. A bolt can heat the air to about **30,000 °C**, around five times hotter than the Sun's surface."],
  ["what is thunder | what causes thunder | why do we see lightning before thunder", "Thunder is the sound of air expanding explosively when lightning heats it. You see lightning first because light travels much faster than sound. Count the seconds between the flash and the boom, then divide by 3 to estimate how many kilometers away it is."],
  ["what should i do in a lightning storm | is it safe to be outside during lightning", "If you hear thunder, you're close enough to be struck. Go indoors or into a hard-topped car and wait about 30 minutes after the last thunder. Avoid open fields, tall isolated trees, water and metal objects."],
  ["what is a tornado | how do tornadoes form", "A tornado is a violently spinning column of air reaching from a thunderstorm down to the ground. They form when warm, moist air meets cool, dry air and winds at different heights cause rotation. The strongest tornadoes can have winds over 300 km/h."],
  ["what is a hurricane | what is the difference between a hurricane and a typhoon | what is a cyclone", "Hurricanes, typhoons and cyclones are all the same kind of giant rotating storm that forms over warm ocean water. The name depends on where it happens: hurricanes in the Atlantic and northeast Pacific, typhoons in the northwest Pacific, and cyclones in the Indian Ocean and South Pacific."],
  ["what is the eye of a hurricane | why is the eye of a storm calm", "The eye is the calm, often clear center of a hurricane, typically 30 to 65 km wide. Air there sinks gently, while the most violent winds swirl in the eyewall right around it."],
  ["what is weather | what is the difference between weather and climate | weather vs climate", "**Weather** is what's happening in the atmosphere right now or over a few days, like rain or sunshine. **Climate** is the average pattern of weather in a place over many years. As a saying goes: climate is what you expect, weather is what you get."],
  ["what is wind | what causes wind", "Wind is moving air. It happens because the Sun heats Earth unevenly, creating areas of high and low air pressure. Air flows from high pressure toward low pressure, and that flow is wind."],
  ["what is the atmosphere | what are the layers of the atmosphere", "The atmosphere is the blanket of gases surrounding Earth. Its main layers, from the ground up, are the **troposphere** (where weather happens), **stratosphere** (home of the ozone layer), **mesosphere**, **thermosphere** and **exosphere**."],
  ["what is the ozone layer | why is the ozone layer important", "The ozone layer is a region in the stratosphere with lots of ozone gas, which absorbs most of the Sun's harmful ultraviolet rays. Chemicals called CFCs once damaged it, but a worldwide ban in 1987 has helped it slowly recover."],
  ["what is climate change | what is global warming | is climate change real", "Climate change is the long-term warming of Earth and the changes in weather patterns that come with it. Scientists agree the main cause today is humans burning coal, oil and gas, which adds greenhouse gases that trap heat. Effects include melting ice, rising seas and more extreme heat waves."],
  ["what is the greenhouse effect | how does the greenhouse effect work | what are greenhouse gases", "The greenhouse effect is when gases like carbon dioxide, methane and water vapor trap some of Earth's heat, like a blanket. Naturally, it keeps Earth warm enough for life. Adding more of these gases makes the blanket thicker, warming the planet."],
  ["how can we stop climate change | what can i do about climate change", "The biggest step is using cleaner energy like solar and wind instead of fossil fuels. Everyday actions help too:\n- Save energy at home\n- Walk, bike or use public transport\n- Waste less food and reuse things\n- Plant trees and protect nature"],
  ["what is renewable energy | types of renewable energy", "Renewable energy comes from sources that naturally refill, like **sunlight, wind, flowing water, geothermal heat** and plant material. Unlike coal, oil and gas, they won't run out and produce little or no greenhouse gas while running."],
  ["how do solar panels work | how does solar power work", "Solar panels are made of cells, usually silicon, that turn sunlight directly into electricity. When light hits the cells, it knocks electrons loose, creating an electric current. This is called the **photovoltaic effect**."],
  ["what are fossil fuels | how are fossil fuels made", "Fossil fuels are coal, oil and natural gas. They formed over millions of years from the buried remains of ancient plants and tiny sea creatures, squeezed and heated underground. Burning them releases energy but also carbon dioxide."],
  ["why is the ocean salty | why is the sea salty | where does the salt in the ocean come from", "Rain slowly dissolves tiny amounts of minerals from rocks on land, and rivers carry them to the ocean. When seawater evaporates, the salt stays behind. Over millions of years, the salt built up. Undersea volcanic vents add some minerals too."],
  ["how much of earth is covered by water | how many oceans are there | what are the five oceans", "About **71%** of Earth's surface is covered by water, and around 97% of that water is in the oceans. The five oceans are the **Pacific, Atlantic, Indian, Southern and Arctic**. The Pacific is the largest and deepest."],
  ["what is the deepest part of the ocean | what is the mariana trench | how deep is the ocean", "The deepest known point is the **Challenger Deep** in the Mariana Trench in the Pacific Ocean, about **11,000 meters** deep. Mount Everest could fit inside with over 2 km to spare. The average ocean depth is about 3,700 meters."],
  ["why is the ocean blue | what color is the ocean", "Water absorbs red, orange and yellow light more than blue, so blue light is scattered back to your eyes. The ocean also reflects some of the sky's blue. Tiny plants, sediment and algae can make it look green or brown in some places."],
  ["what is a coral reef | is coral alive | why are coral reefs important", "Coral reefs are built by tiny animals called coral polyps, which make hard limestone skeletons. Reefs cover less than 1% of the ocean floor but support about a quarter of all marine species. Warming seas can cause coral bleaching, which threatens them."],
  ["who was isaac newton | what did isaac newton discover | what is newton famous for", "**Isaac Newton** (1643–1727) was an English scientist who described the laws of motion and universal gravitation. He also showed that white light is made of colors, built the first practical reflecting telescope, and helped invent calculus."],
  ["who was albert einstein | what did einstein discover | what is einstein famous for", "**Albert Einstein** (1879–1955) was a German-born physicist best known for the theory of relativity and the equation E = mc². He won the 1921 Nobel Prize in Physics for explaining the photoelectric effect, which helped launch quantum physics."],
  ["who was marie curie | what did marie curie discover | what is marie curie famous for", "**Marie Curie** (1867–1934) was a Polish-born French scientist who pioneered research on radioactivity and discovered the elements **polonium** and **radium**. She was the first woman to win a Nobel Prize and the only person to win Nobel Prizes in two different sciences: physics and chemistry."],
  ["who was charles darwin | what did darwin discover | what is darwin famous for", "**Charles Darwin** (1809–1882) was an English naturalist who developed the theory of evolution by natural selection. His observations during the voyage of HMS Beagle, including on the Galápagos Islands, led to his famous 1859 book, *On the Origin of Species*."],
  ["who was galileo | what did galileo discover | why is galileo famous", "**Galileo Galilei** (1564–1642) was an Italian scientist who improved the telescope and used it to discover Jupiter's four largest moons, the phases of Venus and craters on the Moon. His work supported the idea that Earth orbits the Sun, and he's often called the father of modern science."],
  ["who was nikola tesla | what did tesla invent", "**Nikola Tesla** (1856–1943) was a Serbian-American inventor and engineer best known for developing the alternating current (AC) electrical system and the induction motor. He also experimented with radio, wireless power and X-rays."],
  ["who was thomas edison | what did edison invent | did edison invent the light bulb", "**Thomas Edison** (1847–1931) was an American inventor who held over 1,000 patents, including the phonograph and motion picture camera. He didn't invent the first light bulb, but he developed the first practical, long-lasting one and the systems to power it."],
  ["who was copernicus | who said the earth goes around the sun | who discovered the earth orbits the sun", "**Nicolaus Copernicus** (1473–1543) was a Polish astronomer who proposed that the Sun, not Earth, is at the center of the solar system. His idea, published in 1543, transformed astronomy. Ancient Greek thinker Aristarchus had suggested something similar much earlier."],
  ["who was stephen hawking | what did stephen hawking discover", "**Stephen Hawking** (1942–2018) was a British physicist famous for his work on black holes and the origin of the universe. He predicted that black holes slowly give off radiation, now called **Hawking radiation**, and wrote the bestselling book *A Brief History of Time*."],
  ["who was rosalind franklin | what did rosalind franklin do", "**Rosalind Franklin** (1920–1958) was a British chemist whose X-ray images of DNA, especially 'Photo 51', were crucial in revealing its double-helix structure. Her contribution wasn't fully recognized during her lifetime, but she's now celebrated as a key figure in the discovery."],
  ["who was michael faraday | what did faraday discover", "**Michael Faraday** (1791–1867) was an English scientist who discovered electromagnetic induction, the principle behind electric generators and transformers. He also invented an early electric motor, despite having little formal schooling."],
  ["who was louis pasteur | what did pasteur discover | what is pasteurization", "**Louis Pasteur** (1822–1895) was a French scientist who showed that germs cause many diseases and developed vaccines for rabies and anthrax. He also invented **pasteurization**, heating liquids like milk to kill harmful bacteria."],
  ["who was aristotle in science | who was the first scientist", "There's no single first scientist, but ancient thinkers like **Thales** and **Aristotle** in Greece tried to explain nature through observation and reasoning. Later, scholars such as Ibn al-Haytham, around the year 1000, championed testing ideas with experiments, a key part of the modern scientific method."],
  ["who was carl sagan | what is carl sagan famous for", "**Carl Sagan** (1934–1996) was an American astronomer and science communicator who made space exciting for millions through his TV series *Cosmos*. He worked on NASA missions and helped create the Voyager Golden Record."],
  ["who was jane goodall | what is jane goodall famous for", "**Jane Goodall** is a British scientist famous for her decades-long study of wild chimpanzees in Tanzania, beginning in 1960. She discovered that chimps make and use tools, and became a leading voice for animal conservation."],
  ["who was ada lovelace | who was the first computer programmer", "**Ada Lovelace** (1815–1852) was an English mathematician often called the first computer programmer. She wrote what's considered the first algorithm intended for a machine, Charles Babbage's proposed Analytical Engine, and imagined computers doing more than just math."],
  ["what is the scientific method | steps of the scientific method", "The scientific method is how scientists test ideas:\n1. Ask a question\n2. Do background research\n3. Form a hypothesis (a testable prediction)\n4. Run an experiment\n5. Analyze the results\n6. Draw a conclusion and share it"],
  ["what is a hypothesis | difference between hypothesis and theory | what is a scientific theory", "A **hypothesis** is a testable guess or prediction. A scientific **theory** is a well-tested explanation backed by lots of evidence, like the theory of evolution or gravity. In science, 'theory' doesn't mean 'just a guess'."],
  ["what is the hardest natural substance | what is the hardest mineral", "**Diamond** is the hardest natural substance, scoring 10 on the Mohs hardness scale. That's why diamonds are used on saw blades and drill bits to cut through tough materials."],
  ["what is the speed of light in miles | how fast is light in miles per second", "Light travels at about **186,000 miles per second** in a vacuum, which is roughly 670 million miles per hour."],
  ["how many stars are in the universe | how many stars are there", "No one can count them exactly, but astronomers estimate there are roughly **200 billion trillion** (about 10²³) stars in the observable universe. That's more than all the grains of sand on all of Earth's beaches."],
  ["what is the hottest place in the solar system | what is the coldest place in the universe", "The hottest place in the solar system is the Sun's core, at about 15 million °C. The coldest known natural place in the universe is the **Boomerang Nebula**, at about -272 °C, just one degree above absolute zero."]
);
